The **Timms Review** is the first full review of **Personal Independence Payment (PIP)** since the benefit began in 2013. It is named after **Sir Stephen Timms**, the Minister for Social Security and Disability, who co-chairs it.

This article explains what the review covers, how it is being carried out, and what it has found and recommended so far. We will update it as new information is published.

{% callout type="info" title="Nothing has changed yet" %}
The review has **not finished** and its recommendations are **not final**. If you get PIP, your award carries on as normal. Do not let news about the review stop you from claiming PIP or asking for a mandatory reconsideration. Read our [PIP guide](/benefits/pip/).
{% /callout %}

## Why the review is happening

In 2025 the government planned to make it harder to get the daily living part of PIP, with a new rule that you would need at least 4 points in one activity. After strong opposition, this **4-point rule was dropped** in July 2025. Instead, the government promised a wider review of PIP, **co-produced with disabled people**.

The government says it wants PIP to stay a benefit that is:

- **not means-tested**, so your income and savings do not matter
- paid whether you are **in or out of work**
- a **cash** payment towards the extra costs of disability

## What the review covers

The review's **terms of reference** set out what it will look at. It covers the **whole of PIP**, both the daily living part and the mobility part, including people who already get it.

The steering group shaped its work around **4 themes**:

1. **The role and purpose of PIP**, including how it helps people live independently and take part in society
2. **Eligibility, fairness and equity**, including the activities, the wording of the descriptors and the points system
3. **The experience of claiming PIP**, from applying to assessments, decisions and appeals
4. **The changing context**, such as how disability, health and work have changed since PIP began

It is also looking at how PIP can stay **affordable for future generations**, and how the PIP assessment links to other help. The steering group has said it may also comment on wider issues where the evidence shows they affect PIP.

PIP is paid in England, Wales and Northern Ireland. In Scotland, it has been replaced by **Adult Disability Payment**.

## Who is running it

The review is led by **3 co-chairs**:

- **Sir Stephen Timms**, Minister for Social Security and Disability
- **Sharon Brennan**
- **Dr Clenton Farquharson CBE**

Sharon Brennan and Dr Clenton Farquharson joined as co-chairs on 30 October 2025. They lead a **steering group of 12 people**, chosen through an open process from more than 340 applications. Almost all of them have lived experience of disability or long-term ill health.

## How it is being carried out

The review says it is being **co-produced** with disabled people, the organisations that represent them, carers, clinicians and other experts. The government says this is the first time it has used co-production on this scale.

Its work is guided by core principles of **dignity, rights, independent living, equity, accessibility and participation**.

The steering group is using a **6-part plan** to gather evidence, so people can take part in a way that suits them:

1. **A call for evidence**, which ran from 19 March to 28 May 2026 and received **38,713 responses**
2. **Existing data and research**
3. **New survey research**, including a nationally representative survey of working-age disabled people with the National Centre for Social Research
4. **Workshop in a Box**, a free toolkit so community groups and charities can run their own sessions and send in what they hear
5. **Evidence sessions with experts**
6. **Deliberative events**, which are longer, more detailed discussions about options and solutions

The co-chairs publish **regular updates** on GOV.UK, in Easy Read, large print, audio and British Sign Language versions.

## Timeline so far

- **October 2025:** terms of reference published and co-chairs appointed
- **December 2025:** first co-chair update, and steering group members chosen
- **January to February 2026:** steering group induction, then policy work begins
- **19 March to 28 May 2026:** call for evidence
- **June 2026:** Workshop in a Box toolkit launched
- **9 July 2026:** **interim report** published and presented to Parliament
- **15 September to October 2026:** 15 "shaping recommendations" workshops across all 4 UK nations, 9 in person and 6 online
- **Autumn 2026:** final report due to the Secretary of State for Work and Pensions

## What the review has found

The interim report gives a clear message: **PIP is hugely valued**, but its **current design and delivery are no longer fit for purpose**. It says PIP has not kept pace with how disability, health and work have changed.

From the call for evidence:

- **over 90%** of people who described their PIP assessment said it was a **negative** experience, and only about 5% were positive
- the assessment was often described as **stressful and dehumanising**
- many people said the process caused **stress, anxiety and exhaustion**, and sometimes made their condition worse
- the system was described as **complex and hard to navigate**, and decisions as **hard to trust**
- people strongly valued PIP as an essential income that supports **stability, independence and dignity**

## Emerging recommendations

In September 2026 the steering group shared its **emerging recommendations**. These are **not final**. They are a starting point for disabled people to test, challenge and improve at the workshops.

They fall into **3 groups**.

### 1. Improving the experience of claiming

- **fewer unnecessary award reviews** for people with **lifelong or degenerative conditions**, where no improvement is expected
- better processes and communication to **reduce anxiety** about award reviews
- a **fast-track process** for new claimants with a **terminal illness**, and **indefinite awards** for people who qualify under the special rules for end of life
- reviewing how the **28-day hospital rule** works, as PIP is currently suspended after 28 days in a row in hospital, to cut unnecessary stress during serious illness
- **clearer, more respectful letters**, including decision letters
- a better system for asking for **reasonable adjustments**, so they are agreed, delivered and checked

### 2. Reforming assessments and decisions

- **better use of supporting evidence**, with stronger ways to gather, share and consider it
- **learning from mandatory reconsiderations and appeals**, so more decisions are right first time
- a clear **framework** for how decisions are made, challenged and corrected
- stronger **oversight and auditing** of the companies that carry out assessments, and more accountability for assessors

### 3. Building a strong base for the future

- more **accessible digital services**, including an **online portal** for PIP
- **principles** that could guide more fundamental reform of PIP in future, including who should be eligible

The review is clear that PIP should stay a **flexible, non-means-tested cash benefit** towards the extra costs of disability.

## Changes the government has already made

Alongside the review, the government has said that PIP assessments will be **recorded by default**, and that it is increasing **face-to-face assessments** to around 30%.

## What happens next

After the workshops, the steering group will refine its recommendations and send its **final report** to the Secretary of State in **autumn 2026**. It has promised to explain what it took forward from the workshops, and why.

The government will then decide which recommendations to accept. Changes to the PIP activities or points would need new law, so they would not happen straight away.

## What this means for you now

- **Nothing changes yet.** Claim PIP in the usual way if you think you qualify.
- If you already get PIP, **keep reporting changes** to the DWP as normal.
- If you disagree with a decision, you can still ask for a **mandatory reconsideration** and then appeal.
- Check how the activities apply to you with our free [PIP points self-check](/benefits/pip/points-checker/), and read about the [12 PIP activities](/benefits/pip/activities/).
- You can follow every official update on the [Timms Review page on GOV.UK](https://www.gov.uk/government/collections/the-timms-review).

